Strong rules cover Chinese 想 (think), 相 (appearance), and 像/象
(image, likeness). Token-final forms receive more than forms followed by
other characters; compounds receive less than isolated characters.
Related Korean/Vietnamese syllables and split Roman spellings also respond.
Negation and mirror-image contexts adjust the response. Document starts,
interpuncts and title markup have separate corrections. A short byte suffix
completes split characters and spellings; state resets between documents.
This captures a specific spelling family, not all thought or image meanings.
